Per-label notes:
{{ JSON.stringify($('LabelNotes').all().map(i => i.json)) }}

SOURCE TO PROCESS (uploaded file):
{{ $json.chunk_note }}
Sender (provided by uploader): {{ $json.from }}
File: {{ $json.subject }}
Date: {{ $json.date }}
Thread ID: {{ $json.thread_id }}
Uploader context/notes:
{{ $json.body }}

FILE CONTENTS (parsed to Markdown — tables, columns and headings preserved):
ONE SOURCE, SEVERAL RENDERINGS — never output the same release twice. The uploader's notes and
the file contents are the SAME submission extracted in different ways, and the file may present a
release as a table row AND repeat it in running text; its pages arrive as Markdown, or as flat
text only for the pages that could not be rendered as Markdown — never as both. So a
release that shows up more than once is ONE item: emit it ONCE and merge everything known about
it (the version with more detail wins, and a field filled in one place is kept). Two items are
different products only when an identifier (EAN or catalogue number) differs — the same artist,
title and format with different barcodes ARE two items.
In a Markdown table the header row names the columns and EVERY OTHER ROW IS ONE ITEM: read each
row column by column, never merge two rows into one item and never split one row into two. A cell
may be empty — leave the field null rather than borrowing the value from a neighbouring row.
{{ $json.attachments_text }}

